/// clone runs), exactly like `zanshin_git_fetch`'s. With a live `cancel_token` (Story 9.52, a
/// token from `zanshin_network_token`) that id IS the token, so the caller can route the progress
/// before the clone returns; `zanshin_network_cancel` cancels the clone.
///
/// A failure inside the clone's clean-up scope (creating the workspace, adding the remote, the
/// fetch) removes `.jj` (and `.git` when colocated), then the destination iff the call created it;
/// a later failure (the presets, the checkout, the re-open) keeps the workspace — the CLI's scope.
///
/// Returns a `RepoHandle` envelope on success.
///
/// # Errors
/// - `ffi.null_pointer` — `data` is a null pointer.
/// - `ffi.invalid_argument` — `data` does not verify as a `RepoCloneRequest`, its `kind` is not
/// `RepoClone`, or `object_hash` is out of range.
/// - `jj.invalid_input` — `source` or `destination` is absent, both colocation flags are set, a
/// `branches` / `tags` pattern does not parse, or `remote_name` is refused at `add git remote`
/// (reserved `git`, a slash, a name Git rejects; jj-lib's reason in `context`).
/// - `jj.destination_not_empty` — the destination is a file or a non-empty directory; refused
/// before anything is created. The destination in `context`.
/// - `jj.network_error` — the fetch's or the default-branch query's `git` subprocess failed (an
/// unreachable source, an authentication failure, `git` not runnable); the git failure (jj-lib's
/// message with its cause) in `context`.
/// - `jj.cancelled` — `cancel_token` was cancelled (`zanshin_network_cancel`, Story 9.52) before
/// or after the fetch's git work, or git failed after the cancel killed it; inside the clean-up
/// scope, so what the clone created is removed. `context` is "Cancelled". Never without a token.
/// - `jj.jj_lib_error` — the destination cannot be created, or the creation, registering the
/// remote (e.g. an unparseable source URL), another fetch failure (e.g. rejected ref updates),
/// the import or the checkout failed; detail in `context`.
/// - `jj.config_error` — the effective jj config cannot be read, or a key the clone reads is
/// malformed (`git.colocate`, `git.object-hash`, the import options, `remotes.<name>.*`,
/// `git.track-default-bookmark-on-clone`), or the presets cannot be written. `context` names the
/// offending KEY or FILE PATH.
/// - `watcher.io_error` — the filesystem watcher could not be armed. Arming is strict-fail:
/// no handle is returned rather than one for an unwatched repo. It runs AFTER the clone
/// has SUCCEEDED, so the cloned workspace persists and a retry sees a non-empty destination.
/// Detail in `context`.
///
/// # Warnings
/// `Envelope.warnings` carries the clone's warnings on the ok envelope AND on every error
/// envelope, in the CLI's order: the start-up environment's (twice: before and after the remote
/// is added), each finish's (`jj.identity_missing` once per operation written, `jj.git_export_failed`
/// colocated, `jj.working_copy_immutable`, `jj.checkout_files_skipped`, `jj.unresolved_conflicts`),
/// `jj.clone_branches_unmatched` and `jj.git_import_failed` before the fetch's finish; on a
/// failure inside the clean-up scope, `jj.clone_cleanup_failed` last when the clean-up failed.
/// No `Envelope.precondition`.
///
/// # Safety
/// `data` must be null or point to `len` bytes of a caller-owned buffer built and owned/freed by
/// Dart (`fbs.RepoCloneRequestObjectBuilder(...).toBytes()`); Rust only borrows it for the call.
/// The returned token must be freed via `zanshin_buffer_free_token`.
@ffi.Native<ffi.Pointer<ZanshinBuf> Function(ffi.Pointer<ffi.Uint8>, ffi.UintPtr)>()
external ffi.Pointer<ZanshinBuf> zanshin_repo_clone(
  ffi.Pointer<ffi.Uint8> data,
  int len,
);

/// Close an open repository and release its resources.
///
/// `handle` is the opaque integer returned by `zanshin_repo_open`, `zanshin_repo_init`,
/// or `zanshin_repo_clone`. After this call the handle is invalid; the Dart caller
/// must null its local copy.
///
/// Returns a no-payload success `Envelope` on success.
///
/// # Errors
/// - `ffi.invalid_handle` — `handle` is `0` (null).
///
/// # Safety
/// `handle` must be a value previously returned by a successful `zanshin_repo_open`,
/// `zanshin_repo_init`, or `zanshin_repo_clone` call that has not yet been closed.
/// Passing a freed non-zero handle is undefined behaviour; the Dart contract is to
/// null the local handle after `close`. There is no handle table in Rust (AD-2,
/// single-isolate), but the Dart worker's Model-A `handleId` table (Story 3.9) removes
/// the mapping on close, so a stale handle never reaches Rust. The repo handle is a warm
/// loader, not a state cache — reads re-resolve to head (ADR-0002).
/// *(2026-09-30, Story 4.10: `zanshin_commit_contents` is the one pinned read — it answers at
/// the caller-supplied operation and returns commit-intrinsic values only; see the ADR-0002
/// Addendum 2026-09-30.)*
